A: The issue here is that, in unit tests, ArrayList.Insert() actually changes the position of an existing element rather than inserting a new element. In testing context, this doesn't break any fundamental invariants of unit tests perceptions.
However, the issue could be seen in manual testing and debugging. For example, if you observe that the indexing is not as you'd expect after an insert, you may come to a conclusion that your Insert method is flawed. So, if it's intentional to have the same behavior as ArrayList.Insert() during testing, it could be a case of breaking the test if you're using the same code in production.
For better unit test coverage, it would be better to test the effect of ArrayList.Add() separately from the Insert() operation. ArrayList.Add() would introduce a new element and preserve indexing relationships properly during unit testing.
As far as Pex goes, the assumption of [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute could be used to ensure that your method works with its own invariants which may differ from the framework's ArrayList.Insert() method, so Pex will focus on your method's specific behavior.
For production-level code, you might want to reconsider your methods' design as well. Your objective to mimic the behavior of ArrayList.Insert() might be hindering you in achieving good maintainability of your code. You may want to use standard array and length to implement your business logic rather than using ArrayList, unless strictly required.
Hope this clarifies your understanding. Please comment if you need further clarifications.

A: There appears to be a misunderstanding about the use of IndexOf() in unit tests.
Normally, in a unit test, when you change the contents of an object under test (such as inserting an item into an ArrayList), you should look at the index that the new element got inserted at. Not the index of the item before the insert if it existed already.
For example, if you original had items [0,1,2] and you inserted the item 3 at index 1, your index should be checking 1, not 2 (or 0, or 3, etc). This is because indexing starts from 0 and would not change after the insert (otherwise, you wouldn't be able to tell your insertion was happening after the 2 or before the 0, etc).
In your context, if arrList.Insert(index, x) was working as expected, the IndexOf(x) should show the same index it had before the insert even if the item was previously in the array.
I would suggest simplifying your test method to focus on inserting a value at a specific index rather than checking the IndexOf() to ensure the newly inserted value is not affecting existing values' indices. I can provide a corrected code sample if that was your intention.
In summary, unit tests should mimic actual usage, not strictly mimic framework features.
For detailed explanation, let me know.
In conclusion: the code has been corrected so you should have an adjusted test method as:
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index) {
   AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);
   arrList.Insert(index, x);
   Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
   Assert.AreEqual(arrList.Count, oldList.Count + 1);
}

This ensures you are testing if new element is added and also checks if the size of ArrayList is incremented after each Insert operation is executed successfully.
Note: Replace "oldList" in Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x)); with the state of ArrayList pre-insertion i.e., arrList before Insert method is called.
P.s: Just to mention, do not consider the use of ArrayList specifically in your unit tests, it's generally not recommended for such tests. Use arrays or other data structures that can be tested more thoroughly.

