A: I don't think that Microsoft.Pex.Framework allows for constraints on index parameters for the ArrayList.Insert method. As a result, the condition: AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0); in your test is unrealistically high.
The index for the ArrayList.Insert method has to be between 0 and ArrayList.Count. So, you might need to adjust the constraints on the inputs for the tests. 
Here is a sample test which is written for the List class, which has less constraints for indexes compared to ArrayList:
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[PexMethod(ActionUnderTest = "LIST_Insert_Success")]
public void PUT_InsertContractTest(int x, int index)
{       
    // Arrange
    var list = new List<int>() { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= list.Count && index >= 0);

    // Act
    list.Insert(index, x);

    // Assert
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_list_Count", list.Count);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_list_index", index);

    // More assertions here
}

Try to use a class than implements IList or ICollection. In this case the test wouldn't be unrealistically high, but it would still be pretty broad with constraints on the size of tested lists.
If you really need the test coverage for ArrayList you could add this test by using the "Moles" approach to create a wrapper around ArrayList (you can call it MyArrayList) and then use the PexInstrumenter to add additional contracts to your ArrayList methods. But this seems to be too complex and I wouldn't advise it.
Another possibility would be to try MSFxCop or similar tools that focus on static analysis and can be set up to check your code against Microsoft's best practices, which would help you to identify edge cases and other problems. 
In general, I'd suggest to focus more on unit tests as they provide you with the most coverage, then cover the boundary cases manually in a broader tests as long as it's reasonable.

A: You can define input for index with following:
/// <summary>
/// Index lower bound.
/// </summary>
private const int IndexLowerBound = 0;

/// <summary>
/// Index upper bound.
/// </summary>
private const int IndexUpperBound = 10; 

and use it in this way:
[PexMethod]
[PexInAssemblyUnderTest("mscorlib.dll")]
[PexAllowedException]
public void AddOnIndex(
    [PexFactory]
    ArrayList array,
    [PexRange(IndexLowerBound, IndexUpperBound)]
    int index, 
    int valueToAdd)
{
    array.Add(valueToAdd);
    array.Insert(index, valueToAdd);
}

Please note that Pex does not currently support setting values for index parameters of methods like ArrayList.Insert. You can read about limitations here. Please try these modifications and you should get more coverage of your tests.

A: The Pex framework by Microsoft, currently, does not support testing with the Insert method of ArrayList. You can find the list of currently supported methods in this Microsoft Documentation.
ArrayList.Insert() is not among them, hence its not covered by Pex.
You can use other types like List<T> which is a part of .Net Framework and it has better Pex coverage. 
Alternatively, you can create your own wrapper type around ArrayList and try to utilize PexInstrumenter to add additional contracts onto your original ArrayList methods.
However, you might end up with complex and unmanageable tests. It is recommended to use List<T> or similar classes for unit testing which have better Pex coverage. 
Q: how to remove the extra padding of  in the navigation menu I have --------------------	    
        }
    }
}
```

To summarize, `ArrayListContractTest.PUT_InsertContract` performs verification on the `Insert` method of ArrayList. It assumes conditions that are relevant to ArrayList contract and also observes the new states after inserting an object. It also contains a basic Assertion that verifies the index of the object after the insertion operation should be less than or equal to the insertion index.

The PEX test engine will generate various combinations of test cases which it will execute under. In an effort to get green output, the test cases will be designed such that the ArrayList objects and indices always satisfy the preconditions. If the test gets a failure output, it should be able to recognize the cause of failure within the method being tested.


## Summary
C# provides a framework called `Contracts` and tools such as `Pex` which are designed to help developers debug and test code coverage. They can aid in catching issues early and reducing the time spent debugging by providing hints and hints for Pex to consider while performing testing. The examples showed how these tools can be employed to make more informed decisions for testing and code coverage.

Keep in mind that Contracts are contracts within the program logic and they are not contracts of the program’s inputs and outputs. The input/output contracts, meanwhile, are contracts of what the program is expected and expected to do. In general, programs have more than one type of contract, so understanding the different parts of a contract-based testing setup is crucial.


```[edit] (2019, February 13): The example linked in this post shows a contract for a function that adds an element to a specific position in an ArrayList. Since the contract for ArrayList and its methods was not previously shown, consider checking Microsoft's documentation for a full list on the usage and contract guarantee of the ArrayList and its methods.```

